    figure dag aspect 3:2 members ["nD","nY","nU","nA","eDY","eUY","eDA","eUA","boxA","lab_m","lab_m2","gapv","berk","bD","bY","bU","bA","rr","pad","rr2"] hidden true

    card title "What the arrows show"

The hospital trap looks different drawn as arrows: coverage on the left, health on the right, and the arrow (@highlight eDY@) between them is the effect we want.
    draw pad nD nY eDY

Sickness drives health too. Nobody measures it well, but in this illustration the lot (@highlight nU@)tery alone decides coverage, so no arrow joins those two.
    draw nU eUY

Admission to hospital has two causes: coverage makes a bed easier to get, and sickness makes one necessary.
    draw nA eDA eUA

Two arrowheads meet at admission, and a node where arrows collide is called a collider.
    highlight nA

Left alone, a collider blocks the path through it: coverage and sickness both pour into admission, and nothing flows back out.
    flow eDA eUA

Comparing only the admitted draws a box round the collider, and the box opens the path.
    draw boxA

Inside the hospital, an insured patient needed less sickness to get a bed, so coverage now tells you about sickness, and sickness about health.
    highlight eDA eUA eUY

That is where the false five points come from: coverage added fifty healthier admissions per thousand, (@highlight gapv@) and the gap at health reads five.
    draw lab_m lab_m2 gapv

So the leak should grow and shrink with the arrow into admission.
    highlight eDA

Move the number on the arrow into admission down to zero, then up to a hundred and fifty, and watch the gap beside health.
    explore params ["m"] watch "Watch the gap at health shrink to nothing as the arrow into admission weakens, and grow as it strengthens."

With no extra admissions, the gap at health falls to zero: no arrow into the collider, no leak.
    animate m 0 duration 2.5

At a hundred and fifty extra beds, it climbs to ten.
    animate m 150 duration 3

    animate m 50 duration 1

Statisticians met this trap long before economists. In 1946 Joseph Berkson showed that hospital patients can (@highlight boxA@) link two diseases that are unrelated outside.
    draw berk

    hide nD nY nU nA boxA lab_m lab_m2 gapv berk

Its best-known case in medicine is the birth-weight paradox, and it sits on the same four nodes.
    draw bD bA bU bY

In American births from 1991, babies of smokers were more often born small, and more often died in their first year.
    highlight bD bA bY

Yet among the babies born small, those of smokers died les (@draw rr rr2@)s often, by about a fifth: a rate ratio of 0.79.
    highlight bA

The arrows say why: a small baby whose mother did not smoke is more often small for a worse reason, such as a birth defect.
    highlight bU eUA eUY

In 2006 Hernández-Díaz, Schisterman and Hernán showed that collider bias can explain it, with no protective effect of smoking needed.
    highlight rr effect circle

    hide bD bA bU bY rr rr2

    show nD nY nU nA

    quiz "Oregon's researchers wanted the effect of winning the lottery. Which of these could they safely control for?"
        intro: Back to Oregon, with a new choice.
        right: Household size: it was fixed before the draw, so winning cannot move it. Oregon's own analysis controlled for it, because a whole household's chances depended on it.
        wrong: Ask of each one: could winning the lottery have changed it?
        * Doctor visits after the draw
        + Household size on the sign-up form
        * Hospital admission after the draw

The rule, in arrows: never put a box round a collider on a path between treatment and outcome, or round anything the treatment itself moves.
    highlight nA effect box

## Substance or language?
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: pearl2009
      title: 'Causality: Models, Reasoning, and Inference (2nd ed.)'
      authors: Pearl
      year: 2009
      finding: Causal graphs encode assumptions as missing arrows; d-separation decides which variables to condition on, including that conditioning on a collider opens a path.
    - id: imbens2020
      title: 'Potential Outcome and Directed Acyclic Graph Approaches to Causality: Relevance for Empirical Practice in Economics'
      authors: Imbens
      year: 2020
      finding: 'Journal of Economic Literature 58(4): DAGs have shown little practical benefit in settings economists use, and do not easily express shape restrictions such as monotonicity that IV and other designs rely on.'
    - id: pearl2020
      title: On Imbens's Comparison of Two Approaches to Empirical Economics (blog, Causal Analysis in Theory and Practice)
      authors: Pearl
      year: 2020
      finding: 'Replies that monotonicity written in potential outcomes and in a structural model are identical: such restrictions live in the structural equations behind the graph.'
    - id: angrist1996
      title: Identification of Causal Effects Using Instrumental Variables
      authors: Angrist, Imbens and Rubin
      year: 1996
      finding: With an instrument satisfying monotonicity (no defiers), IV estimates the average effect for compliers.
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al.
      year: 2012
      finding: Winning the 2008 lottery raised Medicaid coverage by about 25 percentage points; about 30 percent of those selected enrolled.
takeaways:
    - Controls moved by D add bias
    - 'Arrows: what to control for'
    - 'Potential outcomes: whose effect'

